I think it was due to using the older 1.4 rc1 fdisk. I think the partitions
were not set correctly as BSD.

I could load the system, but after a while, the /usr partition and /var
would
become corrupt, e2fsck would be a real pain complaining about everything
on those partitions.

On this 2004.0 disk, I started with a new BSD (by going to DOS partitions
and back). THEN, I setup the BSD partitions and set them to ext2. I've
had no corruption and what I do to convert them is:

1) Boot 2004.0 cdrom disk
2) tune2fs -j /dev/sda'x'
3) mount /dev/sda2 /mnt/gentoo    (my root partition)
4) change ext2 in fstab for that partition to ext3
5) reboot to the hard disk

I'm not complaining, just guess it required these tricks to get ext3 to work
right for me.

I been running ext3 on Debian and RedHat boxes for years. I even got 2.6.4
to work on my test Debian box. The Redhats, well, they will be converted to
Gentoo now that I see how to load up Gentoo properly (I may just take
my test box, and cookie cutter its partitions to other drives for booting up
my legion of AXP servers :) ).

Thanks!
Carl

