jukka <-@-.-> wrote:
> M�ns Rullg�rd wrote:
> 
>> Just don't expect them to help you if you want to install Compaq's
>> excellent C compiler and speed up programs by as much as 30%.
> 
> BTW is it actually possible to compile everything with ccc, including kernel? 

No.  Kernel was never written in a "Standard C" but in dialect which is
acceptable to gcc.  There is a substantial difference.  OTOH Intel with
icc (for x86) seems currently to be pretty close with this compiler to
accept all gcc specific constructs.

> I'd imagine there are lots of programs that need gcc...

For a resonably written "user space" this should be false or such
programs should be relatively easy to fix.

You will see ccc essential gains (smaller if you are linking with math
libraries from ccc while still compiling with gcc - which is possible
and even easy) in programs which are heavy on a floting point.  If this
is not the case then these gains will be quite reduced and may even turn
out to be losses.  A floating point use in a kernel is a bug.  Besides
if your kernel grabs a significant portion of your machine resources
then you indeed have a serious problem which a compiler replacemnt
is not likely to cure.  OTOH your X server may be worth to benchmark
and a recompilation with ccc should be of the same order of difficulty
as doing that with gcc - one hopes.

   Michal


